# zip — real-world examples

## Basics

```sh
# Create a zip file from files and a directory (-r to recurse)
zip archive.zip file1
zip -r archive.zip directory/

# To list, test and extract zip archives, see unzip:
cheat unzip
```

## Common variations

```sh
# Maximum compression / no compression (store only)
zip -9 -r archive.zip directory/
zip -0 -r archive.zip directory/

# Add files to an existing archive
zip archive.zip newfile.txt

# Update only files that changed (freshen + add)
zip -u archive.zip -r directory/

# Delete a file from an archive
zip -d archive.zip unwanted.txt

# Exclude patterns
zip -r archive.zip project/ -x '*.git*' '*node_modules*'
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Zip up /etc configs on homelab before a zypper dup, dated
sudo zip -r /srv/backups/etc-$(date +%F).zip /etc

# Encrypt with a password prompt (legacy zip crypto — weak; prefer 7z AES)
zip -er secrets.zip ~/Documents/keys/

# Ship a log bundle quietly from a script
zip -q -r /tmp/logbundle.zip /var/log/plexmediaserver/

# Split into 100 MB chunks for an email-size-limited transfer
zip -r -s 100m archive.zip /srv/media/photos/2026/
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-r` | recurse into directories |
| `-9` / `-0` | best / no compression |
| `-u` | update changed files, add new ones |
| `-d` | delete entries from archive |
| `-x PATTERN` | exclude files |
| `-e` | encrypt (prompts for password) |
| `-q` | quiet |
| `-s SIZE` | split archive (e.g. `100m`) |
